    "content": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "is wav the only recordin format available? I am concerned that long sessions from multiple streamns could eat up a lot of space as WAV"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "role": "assistant",
    "timestamp": "2026-08-15T17:59:41.227Z",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "text": "Good instinct — let's put real numbers on it, then fix it.\n\n**WAV sizes (16-bit PCM, 48 kHz):** mic (mono) ≈ **345 MB/hour**; an app like Chrome/Zoom (stereo) ≈ **690 MB/hour**. A 2-hour meeting with Chrome + Zoom + mic ≈ **2.4 GB**. Survivable, but across many sessions it piles up fast — fair concern.\n\nWAV isn't the only option though. Native alternatives at *record* time:\n\n**A)** **ALAC (Apple Lossless) stems — my recommendation.** Core Audio encodes it in real time natively (`ExtAudioFile` writes ALAC just like WAV). Typically **~40–60% of WAV size**, zero quality loss, and the export engine reads it back transparently — mixing works exactly the same. Slightly more CPU during recording, negligible on any modern Mac.\n\n**B)** **WAV 16-bit stems** — simplest possible code path, sizes as above. Fine if sessions are short.\n\n**C)** **AAC stems** — smallest (~1 MB/min, ~90% smaller than WAV), but lossy, and re-encoding after mixdown compounds it slightly. For a transcription pipeline it'd be fine in practice, but stems are your \"never lose anything\" archive, so lossless is the safer default.\n\nAnd one cheap complement regardless of format: **\"delete stems after export\"** — once your friend has exported their M4A mix, offer to trash the stems automatically. Session folder drops from gigabytes to a ~60 MB M4A they keep.\n\nMy proposal: **default A (ALAC), with the format as a setting** (ALAC / WAV), plus the post-export stem-cleanup prompt. If a friend ever needs bit-exact WAV stems they flip the setting; everyone else gets half-size lossless archives.\n\nDoes that resolve the space concern? If yes — with this folded in — do I have your approval on Part 1 (architecture), so I can move to Part 2 (UI + edge cases)?"
      }
    ],
    "model": "glm-5.3",
    "stopReason": "stop"
  }
]

